English lessons today's mini English  lesson is about verbs verbs followed by  other verbs verbs can be followed by the  infinitive to plus the base form to eat  for example or Gerrans  verb plus the ing eating so some verbs  are always followed by gerunds enjoy I  enjoy eating fresh croissants in the  morning and some verbs are always  followed by the infinitive I want to eat  eggs in the bacon today want is an  example of a verb always followed by the  infinitive but how do we know which ones  to use unfortunately for these verbs you  just have to learn them sorry  then there are a group of verbs that can  be followed by gerunds infinitives these  verbs a little bit more complicated  because there is a slight difference in  meaning I like oh I love eating  chocolate  or I love to eat chocolate with my  coffee in the morning if we say I love  eating it's more general but if I say I  love to eat then our specific moment the  last group of verbs are verbs that can  be followed by gerunds or infinitives  but the meaning changes quite a lot stop  stop  is an example that can be used by both  the difference is quite big I stopped  eating so much cake because I was  worried about my weight and I stopped to  eat another piece of cake here how do we  know what the difference in meaning is I  stopped eating we are looking backwards  at the event where as I start to eat is  looking forwards at the event let's just  recap there are three different types of  verbs that can be followed by other  verbs the first example we looked at  there are verbs that are always followed  by gerunds are always followed by  infinitives then there are those verbs  like lie and love  which are sometimes followed by one or  the other there is a little difference  in meaning but basically they are very  similar then we have those verbs like  stop who are very different meanings if  we use them with gerunds or follow them  by infinitives I think you should  remember to stop before you use a gerund  or infinitive after reverb next time  [Music]
 

